<p>
That didn&rsquo;t take long. After <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=13681">yesterday&rsquo;s post</a> on adapting Bozhidar Batsov&rsquo;s <code>delete-surrounding-pair</code> function to handle Org markup, Batsov is back with a <a href="https://emacsredux.com/blog/2026/03/17/surround-el-vim-style-pair-editing-comes-to-emacs/">post on surround.el</a>, a <a href="https://github.com/mkleehammer/surround">port of surround.vim</a> from Michael Kleehammer. The point of <code>delete-surrounding-pair</code> was to (sort of) emulate Vim&rsquo;s surround plugin. Now with <code>support.el</code>, there&rsquo;s no longer a need for <code>delete-surrounding-pair</code>.
</p>
<p>
Surround does everything <code>delete-surrounding-pair</code> did and a lot more. You can delete surrounding pairs, add pairs, change pairs, mark or kill the content between the pairs, or even kill the text and the surrounding pair. It really is a splendid package and I&rsquo;ve already added it to my configuration and deleted my adaption of <code>delete-surrounding-pair</code>.
</p>
<p>
Surround will check if the pair you&rsquo;re operating on has different opening and closing characters and do the right thing automatically. The other nice thing is that there aren&rsquo;t a lot of key sequences to remember. Once you pick a prefix that you&rsquo;ll remember the (single) action keys are mnemonic and easy to remember. In any event, <code>which-key</code> pops up a reminder.
</p>
<p>
I installed it with the recommended prefix of <kbd>Meta</kbd>+<kbd>&rsquo;</kbd> but keep wanting to invoke it with <kbd>Hyper</kbd>+<kbd>&rsquo;</kbd> so I&rsquo;ll probably change it but, regardless, I&rsquo;m already enjoying using it.
</p>
<p>
Take a look at Batsov&rsquo;s post or the Github repository for all the details. Batsov&rsquo;s post has some recommendations on when you should use it and Kleehammer&rsquo;s repository has information on shortcuts and auto mode, which you should read. This is a really useful package and I recommend that everyone take a look at it.</p>
